ME.md"], "calls": [["Bash", "ls -la && git ls-files | head -50 && find . -name 'events.csv' -not -path './.git/*' | head"], ["Bash", "cat events.csv; echo ---; cat README.md"], ["Skill", "zz-timezone-audit-10492"]], "final_message": "Mixed timezone handling in `events.csv` is the likely cause. The file has three rows, and each uses a different convention.\n\n**Classification by offset**\n\n| id | ts | offset | UTC equivalent |\n|----|----|--------|----------------|\n| 1 | `2026-03-01T10:00:00Z` | UTC (`Z`) | 10:00 |\n| 2 | `2026-03-01T10:05:00` | none | unknown |\n| 3 | `2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00` | -06:00 | 10:10 |\n\n**Rows with no offset:** id 2.\n\n**Why the charts jump**\n\n- Rows 1 and 3 are 10 minutes apart in real time. Read at face value, their wall-clock times are 10:00 and 04:10, which is a 6-hour gap.\n- Row 2 has no offset, so each tool has to guess. Pandas and JS `Date` may treat it as UTC or as local time, depending on the library and the machine's timezone. Charts built on different machines can then put the row several hours apart. Row 2 is probably meant to be about 10:05Z, between the other two, but the file doesn't say.\n- Any step that drops the offset instead of converting (for example, truncating to `YYYY-MM-DDTHH`) would put row 3 at 04:00 and row 1 at 10:00.\n\n**Fixes**\n\n1. Convert every timestamp to UTC before bucketing, for example `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)`.\n2. Find out what timezone the source of row 2 uses. Then localize it explicitly rather than letting the library guess. If it's UTC, it's 10:05Z.\n3. Make the exporter write an explicit offset on every row.\n\nI haven't changed any files. I can patch the chart code or normalize the CSV if you tell me the timezone for the naive rows. I haven't seen the chart code yet, so point me to it if you want me to check how it parses `ts`.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}